{
  "id": "py-18-walrus",
  "track": "python",
  "unit": "py-u3",
  "order": 18,
  "title": "Assign and test at once: the walrus :=",
  "summary": "Store a value inside a test, so a loop can read and check in one line.",
  "language": "python",
  "minutes": 15,
  "visual": "memory",
  "visuals": [
    "expr",
    "stdin",
    "loop"
  ],
  "intro": {
    "text": "A loop that reads answers until the user types quit often has to call input() twice: once before the loop and once at the end of the block. The **walrus operator** `:=` (it looks like a walrus on its side) fixes that.\n\n`name := value` stores the value in the name **and** gives the value back, so it can sit inside a test:\n\n```\nwhile (word := input(\"Word? \")) != \"quit\":\n    print(word)\n```\n\nEach round, Python asks for a word, stores it in `word`, then compares it with \"quit\". The brackets are needed: without them the comparison would happen first.\n\nA plain `=` cannot go inside a test, and `:=` cannot be used on a line of its own. Use the walrus when it removes a repeated line; otherwise plain `=` is clearer. It arrived in Python 3.8, after the book.",
    "analogy": "Reading a parcel's label while you put it on the shelf, in one move, instead of putting it down first and then bending over to read it."
  },
